All 5 pages are now rewritten with consistent structure. Let me now verify the CSS has all the necessary styles and fix any remaining issues. Let me also check for any CSS classes that might be missing.

Let me review the CSS file to ensure it covers all the elements used in the HTML files. Key things to check:
1. The `featured-banner` styling - I wrapped it in a section with padding
2. The `hero-label` - yes it's in CSS
3. The `highlight` class in hero h1 - yes it's in CSS
4. The `integration-content` class - yes it's in CSS
5. The `sr-only` class was used in old integration.html but not anymore

Let me check a few specific things:
- The featured banner needs the `<h3>` and `<span>` styles. Looking at the CSS, `.featured-banner h3` and `.featured-banner .banner-tagline` are styled. Good.
- The hero section uses `.container` now. The CSS `.hero` has padding but doesn't have a `.hero-inner` style. Good.
- Cookie banner uses `.hidden` class now. Looking at CSS, `.cookie-banner.hidden` has `transform: translateY(100%)`. Good.

One issue I notice: the featured banner in the CSS has padding and max-width set on `.featured-banner` itself, but I wrapped it in a section with padding: 0 24px. The banner has `max-width: var(--container)` and `margin: 0 auto` in CSS. But I also have it inside a section. This should be fine since the section just provides horizontal padding.

Actually wait, the CSS has `.featured-banner` with `margin: 0 auto; max-width: var(--container);` which is fine. The wrapping section just provides left/right padding which will be overridden by the banner's own max-width. Actually the section `style="padding: 0 24px 0;"` will add horizontal padding but the banner itself already has padding. This should work fine visually.

Let me also check if the accordion body needs an initial max-height for the open state. In the platform-overview.html JS, I set `body.style.maxHeight = body.scrollHeight + 'px'` for initially open items. This is correct for the CSS transition on max-height.

But wait, there's an issue with the accordion. The CSS has:
```css
.accordion-body {
  max-height: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  transition: max-height 0.35s ease;
}
```

And the first item has class `open` but the body doesn't have `max-height` set inline. The JS sets it on page load, but only if the item has `.open` class. Let me check the platform-overview.html script... yes, it does set `body.style.maxHeight = body.scrollHeight + 'px'` for items with `.open` class. Good.

But there's a subtle issue - the JS runs after the page loads, but the first accordion item body might not have rendered its content yet, so `scrollHeight` might be 0. Actually no, the JS runs synchronously at the bottom of the body, after the DOM is parsed. The content should be available. But the body has `max-height: 0` and `overflow: hidden` so scrollHeight might still report the correct height since the content is in the DOM even if hidden.

Actually, let me think about this more carefully. `scrollHeight` reports the height of the content including content not visible on the screen due to overflow. So even with `max-height: 0` and `overflow: hidden`, `scrollHeight` should report the full content height. This is correct.

Let me also verify the CSS handles the `section-alt` background properly for sections that use it. The CSS has `.section-alt { background: var(--bg-secondary); }`. Good.

Now let me think about what else might need fixing:

1. The `.featured-banner` in index.html - I used a section wrapper with inline style. This could be cleaner. Let me check if it renders properly.

2. The accordion on platform-overview - the initial open item might not show its content properly because the JS runs at page load but the CSS has `max-height: 0`. Actually, looking more carefully, the first accordion item has class `open` and the body doesn't have `hidden` attribute. But the CSS `.accordion-body` has `max-height: 0` by default. The JS sets `body.style.maxHeight = body.scrollHeight + 'px'` for items with `.open`. So the initial state should be: body has `max-height: 0` from CSS, then JS runs and sets `max-height` to the content height. This should work.

Actually wait, there's a conflict. The accordion-body for the first item doesn't have `hidden` attribute, but the CSS gives it `max-height: 0`. The JS then sets `max-height` to the scroll height. This means there will be a brief flash where the content is hidden then shown. Let me add a CSS rule for `.accordion-item.open .accordion-body` to handle this.

Let me add to the CSS:
```css
.accordion-item.open .accordion-body {
  max-height: 500px; /* fallback */
}
```

This ensures the open accordion item's body is visible even before JS runs.

3. I should also check that the testimonial carousel works. The CSS has:
```css
.testimonial-track {
  display: flex;
  transition: transform 0.5s ease;
}
.testimonial-card {
  min-width: 100%;
}
```

And the JS uses `track.style.transform = 'translateX(-' + (currentSlide * 100) + '%)'`. This should work correctly.

4. The cookie banner - the CSS has `.cookie-banner.hidden` with `transform: translateY(100%); pointer-events: none;`. And the JS adds `.hidden` class. Good.

5. Let me verify the `.nav.open` class works for mobile. The CSS has:
```css
@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .nav { display: none; }
  .nav.open { display: flex; ... }
}
```

And the JS toggles `.open` on `.nav`. But the nav element is `<nav class="nav" id="mainNav">`. The JS does `mainNav.classList.toggle('open')`. Good.

But wait, the nav element is a `<nav>` not a flex container by default. The `.nav` CSS has `display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;`. So on desktop, the nav is already flex. On mobile, it's `display: none` then `display: flex` when `.open`. But the mobile `.nav.open` also sets `flex-direction: column` and other styles. This should work.

6. The dropdown menu in the nav - on mobile, the CSS has:
```css
.nav.open .nav-dropdown .dropdown-menu {
  position: static;
  opacity: 1;
  visibility: visible;
  ...
}
```

This makes the dropdown always visible when the mobile nav is open. Good.

I think the main remaining issue is adding the CSS for the open accordion state. Let me also add a few missing styles.

Actually, let me also think about the `featured-banner` section wrapper. Instead of using inline style on a section, I should just place the banner directly after the hero section and let the `.featured-banner` class handle its own positioning. But the CSS has `max-width: var(--container)` and `margin: 0 auto` on the banner, so it will be centered. The issue is that it's inside a section with padding. Let me remove the wrapper section and just place the banner directly.

Actually, looking at the HTML structure:
```html
<section style="padding: 0 24px 0;">
    <div class="featured-banner">...</div>
</section>
```

The banner already has `padding: 40px 48px` and `max-width: var(--container)` and `margin: 0 auto`. The section wrapper just adds 24px horizontal padding, which would make the banner narrower on small screens. This is actually fine for responsive purposes. But the section doesn't have any class for vertical padding, so there's no vertical spacing between the hero and the banner. Let me add some bottom margin or padding.

Actually, the hero section has `padding: 140px 0 var(--section-pad)` which is `padding: 140px 0 80px`. So there's already 80px of bottom padding after the hero content. The banner appears after the hero section with the section wrapper providing some additional spacing. This should look fine.

Let me now add the accordion CSS fix and make any other small tweaks to the CSS.

